Use:  kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp --to-revision=1

Example:
Revision 1 → nginx:1.25
Revision 2 → nginx:1.26
Revision 3 → nginx:1.27   ← current

Run:  kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp --to-revision=1
Then:  Revision 1 → nginx:1.25   ← restored


Important difference
kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp
→ Goes back to the previous revision (3 → 2).

kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp --to-revision=1
→ Goes specifically to revision 1 (3 → 1).

You can first check the revisions with:  kubectl rollout history deployment/myapp

kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp --to-revision=1
Kubernetes then automatically performs the transition back to that revision.
------------------------

kubectl rollout
       │
       ├── status   → see current rollout status
       ├── history  → see rollout history/revisions
       ├── undo     → rollback to previous revision
       ├── pause    → pause a rollout
       └── resume   → resume a rollout

1. Pause the rollout
kubectl rollout pause deployment/myapp
This tells Kubernetes:  Stop processing further changes to this Deployment for now.

2. Resume the rollout
kubectl rollout resume deployment/myapp
This tells Kubernetes:  Continue processing the Deployment again.
