Here is a complete Deployment example.

1. Initial Deployment — v1
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: myapp
spec:
  replicas: 3

  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxUnavailable: 1
      maxSurge: 1

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: myapp

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: myapp
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: myapp
          image: myapp:v1
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080

Initially:

Deployment
    |
    +---- Pod 1 → myapp:v1
    +---- Pod 2 → myapp:v1
    +---- Pod 3 → myapp:v1


2. Rolling Update — v1 → v2

You change only the image:

image: myapp:v2

For example:

kubectl set image deployment/myapp myapp=myapp:v2

Kubernetes doesn't delete all 3 old Pods at once.

Instead, it gradually replaces them:

Old:
Pod 1 → v1
Pod 2 → v1
Pod 3 → v1

Then:

Pod 1 → v2    NEW
Pod 2 → v1
Pod 3 → v1

Then:

Pod 1 → v2
Pod 2 → v2    NEW
Pod 3 → v1

Finally:

Pod 1 → v2
Pod 2 → v2
Pod 3 → v2

This is a rolling update.



3. Rollback — v2 → v1

Suppose v2 has a problem.
You can check Deployment history:  kubectl rollout history deployment/myapp
Then rollback:  kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp

Kubernetes changes it back:

Before rollback:
Pod 1 → v2
Pod 2 → v2
Pod 3 → v2

Rollback starts:

Pod 1 → v1    NEW
Pod 2 → v2
Pod 3 → v2

Then:

Pod 1 → v1
Pod 2 → v1    NEW
Pod 3 → v2

Finally:

Pod 1 → v1
Pod 2 → v1
Pod 3 → v1

So:

v1
 ↓
Rolling Update
 ↓
v2
 ↓
Problem
 ↓
Rollback
 ↓
v1
Important

The Deployment YAML doesn't normally contain a separate rollback: true setting.

The Deployment's:

strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate

controls how updates happen.

Rollback is performed using the Kubernetes command:  kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp

And you can see the current rollout with:  kubectl rollout status deployment/myapp
Simple interview answer:
Rolling update gradually replaces old Pods with new Pods during a Deployment update. Rollback returns the Deployment to a previous revision when the new version has a problem.


Even if you don't write strategy: in the Deployment YAML, changing the image will still normally perform a rolling update.
Why? Because RollingUpdate is the default Deployment strategy.


Later you change:  image: myapp:v2
Kubernetes sees that the Pod template changed, creates a new ReplicaSet, and performs a rolling update.

v1 ReplicaSet
   ↓
Pod 1 → v1
Pod 2 → v1
Pod 3 → v1

        ↓ change image to v2

v2 ReplicaSet
   ↓
Pod 1 → v2
Pod 2 → v2
Pod 3 → v2
Then why do we write this?
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxUnavailable: 1
    maxSurge: 1

Because you want to control the rolling update behavior.
For example:  maxUnavailable: 1

means Kubernetes can have at most 1 fewer available Pod than the desired number.

maxSurge: 1
means Kubernetes can temporarily create 1 extra Pod above the desired number.

So with:
replicas: 3
maxUnavailable: 1
maxSurge: 1

you can temporarily have:
4 Pods
during the update.

Very important
If you don't specify the strategy:

strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate

Kubernetes uses RollingUpdate by default, with its default values for maxUnavailable and maxSurge.
So you don't need to write strategy just to get a rolling update. You write it when you want to customize the update behavior.


---------------------------------------------------
The easiest way is to see two possible strategies.

maxSurge: 1

Kubernetes creates the new Pod first.

You have 3 Pods:

v1  v1  v1

Create one extra v2:

v1  v1  v1  v2

Now v2 is ready:

v1  v1  v2

So maxSurge answers:

"How many EXTRA Pods can I create?"

maxUnavailable: 1

Instead, Kubernetes can remove an old Pod first.

Start:

v1  v1  v1

Remove one:

v1  v1  ❌

Now only 2 Pods are available.

Then create v2:

v1  v1  v2

So maxUnavailable answers:

"How many Pods am I allowed to temporarily lose while updating?"

Why have both?

Because Kubernetes needs flexibility.

maxSurge: 1
     ↓
Can temporarily ADD 1 Pod

maxUnavailable: 1
     ↓
Can temporarily REMOVE/lose 1 Pod

For example, with:

replicas: 3
maxSurge: 1
maxUnavailable: 1

Kubernetes can operate between roughly:

Minimum available: 2
Maximum Pods:      4

So:

          3 desired
             |
      ┌──────┴──────┐
      ↓             ↓
   -1 Pod         +1 Pod
      ↓             ↓
maxUnavailable   maxSurge

Important: maxSurge does not mean "replace the Pod." It only says how many additional Pods can exist temporarily. maxUnavailable says how many can be unavailable while the replacement happens.


----------------------------
Let's use exactly your example.

You have:

3 Pods

A(v1)  B(v1)  C(v1)

You set:

maxSurge: 1

Kubernetes can create one extra Pod:

A(v1)  B(v1)  C(v1)  D(v2)

Now there are 4 Pods.

But your Deployment says:

replicas: 3

You don't want 4 Pods permanently.

So Kubernetes must eventually remove an old v1 Pod:

A(v1)  B(v1)  D(v2)

Now there are 3.

Then:

A(v1)  D(v2)  E(v2)

Then:

D(v2)  E(v2)  F(v2)

Finally all 3 are v2.


now your question is specifically: why do we need maxUnavailable at all if maxSurge can handle the update?

We need maxUnavailable because sometimes we don't want to create extra Pods.

Case 1: maxSurge: 1

You have 3 Pods:

v1  v1  v1

Kubernetes can create an extra Pod:

v1  v1  v1  v2

Then remove an old one:

v1  v1  v2

✅ No need to make a Pod unavailable.

Case 2: maxSurge: 0

Now suppose you cannot create an extra Pod.

maxSurge: 0
maxUnavailable: 1

You have:

v1  v1  v1

Kubernetes must first remove one old Pod:

v1  v1  ❌

Then create the new one:

v1  v1  v2

So for this method, you need:

maxUnavailable: 1

because Kubernetes is allowed to temporarily have one less available Pod.

So the real reason

There are two choices:

              Update Pods
                  |
       ┌──────────┴──────────┐
       ↓                     ↓
 Create extra Pod       Remove old Pod first
       ↓                     ↓
 maxSurge: 1          maxUnavailable: 1

maxSurge = "I have room/resources for an extra Pod."

maxUnavailable = "I don't have/want extra Pods, so I allow one Pod to be temporarily unavailable."



